<commonOcean timeStepSize="1.0" commonOceanVersion="2022a" author="hybraut_nav_colreg_sim" affiliation="hand-authored demo scenario, not real AIS data" source="synthetic" benchmarkID="COLREG_MULTI_WAYPOINT-1" date="2026-08-18">
  <!-- Multi-waypoint strategic route, one distinct COLREGs encounter per leg: ego cruises at 7.7 m/s (15 kn hydrofoil), dispatched by strategy_node one leg at a time via three <planningProblem> goalStates (see hybraut_nav_colreg_sim/scenario_loader.py's ego_goal_waypoints - only the first <planningProblem> needs an <initialState>, the rest just add another leg). Leg 1: (0,0) -> (0,600), heading north - obstacle 200 crosses from the ego's starboard side (Rule 15), same geometry as COLREG_CROSSING_STARBOARD-1.xml, reaching the ego's track (x=0) at t=38.96s. Leg 2: (0,600) -> (300,600), heading east - obstacle 201 is a stopped vessel dead ahead that the ego must overtake (Rule 13); being static (not scripted to a specific time), this leg is insensitive to exactly when it starts, unlike a timed crossing. Leg 1's endpoint/leg 2 are deliberately at y=600, not y=400 (nearer obstacle 200's own y=300 crossing line) - riskenv's obstacle-obstacle proximity check (calc_I2) folds any two obstacles within dsf of *each other* into one shared unsafe region regardless of the agent, so a leg-2 obstacle left within dsf of leg-1's obstacle enlarges leg 1's own unsafe region even before leg 2 starts; y=600 keeps the closest approach between the two obstacles just over 300m, safely clear of dsf. Leg 3: (300,600) -> (300,50), heading south - obstacle 202 crosses slowly (Rule 15) around t=120s on its own long westbound run, giving a wide window that tolerates the cumulative timing drift from any avoidance manoeuvring on legs 1-2. A head-on (Rule 14) first leg was tried and dropped: at current tactical_node defaults a fast-closing head-on can trigger e3 (reroute) and e2 (Fallback) within the same ~0.2s, before the cascaded reference/PID heading loops have had time to actually turn the vessel - Fallback then holds that still-nearly-head-on heading at full speed (immediate_node doesn't reduce real speed for Fallback), so the two vessels can stay within safety_radius long enough to trip fallback_timeout. Left as a known follow-up (needs either a Fallback flow that actively deconflicts, or immediate_node honouring Fallback's braking intent) rather than papered over with more guesswork on this file's own numbers. -->
